From 9ba4fd8618ab091cb92605ae253c9bbf0fe56978 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Macktherealonereally <macdev@mackthessd.org>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 22:29:51 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] aidl: camera: Expose Samsung high frame rate video modes

Samsung Exynos camera HALs only publish fps ranges up to 30 fps in
android.control.aeAvailableTargetFpsRanges. Their per-request check
(ExynosCameraConfigurations::m_adjustPreviewFpsRange) accepts a range
with a maximum between 31 and 119 fps only if it is part of that same
sensor table, or if the session was configured by a Samsung client
through the samsung.android.control.cameraClient session parameter.
Otherwise the HAL silently keeps the previous range, so apps asking for
[60, 60] record at 30 fps. On AOSP no Samsung session parameter ever
reaches the HAL anyway: android.request.availableSessionKeys is empty,
so the framework filters all of them out.

The real video modes are listed in the vendor tag
samsung.android.scaler.availableVideoConfigurations (width, height,
min fps, max fps, ...). When samsungCameraVars.high_fps_video is set:

 - Add the fixed high fps modes of that table (e.g. [60, 60]) to
   aeAvailableTargetFpsRanges and lower the minimum frame durations of
   the matching PRIVATE/YUV sizes.
 - Make CONTROL_AE_TARGET_FPS_RANGE a session key, so the framework
   passes it to configure_streams and reconfigures the session when an
   app switches to or from a high fps range.
 - For sessions with such a range, add cameraClient
   (samsungCameraVars.high_fps_video_client, default 2: Samsung SDK
   client) and recordingMinFps/recordingMaxFps to the session
   parameters, like the stock camera stack does.
 - Only ask for a reconfiguration when the effective high fps range
   changes, so switching between normal ranges stays seamless.

Sessions at 30 fps or below and constrained high speed sessions are
left untouched. The vendor tags are resolved by name, and nothing
changes if the HAL does not provide them.

Tested on a Galaxy S21 (SM-G991B, Exynos 2100) with Aperture: 1080p60
on the rear and front cameras records 60.0 fps (mean frame interval
16.66 ms, no gaps over 25 ms); 30 fps recording is unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Change-Id: I3f677f24054191fdb0976ea8ee4a66a9b0f4de76
